cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use thermify in research, please cite the software as below."
title: "thermify: Efficient Agroclimatic Thermal Indices"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Fuentes-Jaque
    given-names: Guillermo
    orcid: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7864-4899
    email: gfuentesj@correo.uss.cl
    affiliation: >-
      Facultad de Ingeniería, Universidad San Sebastián, Chile;
      Instituto de Ciencias y Tecnología Ambiental ICTA Ltda., Chile
abstract: >-
  thermify is an R package for efficient, reproducible and spatially explicit
  agroclimatic thermal indices: growing degree-days (average, single and
  double sine, single and double triangle, with horizontal, vertical and
  intermediate cutoffs), degree-hours, chilling hours, Utah model chill units
  and Dynamic model chill portions. It computes them from hourly, daily or
  monthly temperatures; monthly climatologies such as WorldClim 2.1 are
  densified with a mean-preserving method (natively or through tempify) and
  disaggregated to hours, and rasters are processed in blocks with terra.
keywords:
  - agroclimatology
  - growing degree-days
  - chilling hours
  - chill portions
  - dynamic model
  - utah model
  - phenology
  - worldclim
  - raster
  - R
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/djwillichile/thermify"
url: "https://djwillichile.github.io/thermify/"
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-10-31"
references:
  - type: software
    title: "tempify: Temporal densification for geospatial raster stacks"
    authors:
      - family-names: Fuentes-Jaque
        given-names: Guillermo S.
